    "methodology": "For every reported curve point p (front fi, time t_k) with unit normal n, the reconstructed field is sampled bilinearly at p - delta*n and p + delta*n (delta = 0.30, far outside the fitted transition width, so both samples sit on the plateaus). On each side the residual compares that reconstructed sample with the median of raw observations in the corresponding tube: tangential offset |(p_obs-p).d_hat| <= 0.5 along the curve direction d_hat=(-b,a), normal offset rel = n.p_obs - (gamma_fi + c_fi*t_obs) in [delta-0.2, delta+0.2] (+ side) or its negative (- side), |rel| > 0.1 (transition zone excluded), and |t_obs - t_k| <= 0.08. Because the fronts translate along n, this side assignment is exact within the time window. The per-point residual is the mean of the two one-sided deviations |u_obs_side - u_recon_side|; pass_fraction_20pct is the fraction of points whose residual is below 20% of that front's expected jump |u(+n)-u(-n)|. Sign convention: n = (a,b) points toward increasing s = n.p; signed normal speed = d(n.p)/dt = c_k (negative => motion toward -n). Note: t_5 = 0.4637 slightly exceeds the last observation time (0.46318); the linear front tracks make this a negligible extrapolation."
